  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nMOUNTAIN SNOW AND VALLEY RAIN RETURNS:\n\nMultiple troughs are anticipated to make their way through the \nIntermountain West over the coming week, bringing snowfall to \nthe mountains and rain showers to valleys. Presently, two \ntroughs are situated to our west, one offshore of the PacNW and \none skirting along the southwestern border of the US. The former\nwill carry with it a moderate surge of AR moisture, most of \nwhich is expected to get held up on the Sierra Nevada. The \nlatter is delivering its own pool of potent moisture as well. \nEnsembles are keying in on both sources of moisture transiting \nthe southwestern CONUS and into our CWA, arriving before \nmidnight tonight. As this plume pushes through the warmer \nvalleys, rain showers are expected to develop in the Four \nCorners region and up towards east-central Utah. By midnight, \nstorms will be climbing into the San Juan mountains where \ntemperatures will be below freezing, hence snow will dominate. \nSnow levels at the onset of this moisture are hovering around \n9000 feet before fropa near sunset drops them another thousand \nfeet and quickly cooling overnight temperatures bring them down \nto 6000-7000 feet by the end of the event early Sunday morning. \nThe strongest snow rates are expected to hit in the early to \nmiddle portion of the event though, likely between 6 AM and \nnoon, and so the bulk of impactful accumulations are likely \nabove 9000 feet. As it stands, above this elevation, \naccumulations are expected to rest between 4-8 inches in the San\nJuans (and below 4 inches in the central Colorado mountains and\nGrand Mesa), though it is worth noting that the NBM 90th \npercentile is still suggesting that accumulations over a foot in\nthe San Juans at pass level and above are not improbable. \nStill, given the consistency between forecasts up to this point,\nwe see the likeliest scenario being widespread accumulations \nunder a foot with only localized heavier snowfall exceeding this\nthreshold. Regardless, given the expected snowfall and this \npossibility of higher accumulations, conditions have warranted a\nwinter weather advisory, which has been issued from midnight \ntonight through 9 PM Saturday for the southern slopes of the San\nJuans over 9000 feet. Given the lack of snowfall during \novernight periods, it is possible that snow will struggle to \nstick to roadways, so travel impacts may be somewhat localized \nthroughout the day tomorrow. Regardless, it is imperative to be\nweather aware and be cautious should you be driving through the\nmountains tomorrow.\n\nPrecipitation lulls beginning Sunday afternoon and through \nMonday before another wave descends into the western CONUS on \nTuesday. Early indications are for similar moisture content to \naccompany this system, but the overall complexion of the storm \nis still uncertain. Models are depicting a strong front to \naccompany the wave, but its passage does not currently align \nwell enough with the heaviest precipitation to support higher \nsnow water ratios. However, given the complex nature of the \nupper-level pattern at this time, it's uncertain if the timing \nof the moisture and fropa will hold to this timeline or shift. \nThis would have notable implications on snow accumulations, and \nso we cannot speculate on that detail either at this time. We \nare confident that there will be active weather at this time \nthough, so it is safe to plan for potentially impactful \nprecipitation at some point during Tuesday and Wednesday. \nFurthermore, there is signal of an additional wave moving in \nquickly behind this one. Additional details on precipitation \nwith that wave will be elaborated on when model guidance becomes\nclearer.",
